 ## Architecture
 
-The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
+The record store exposes authenticated submission, visit retrieval, revision-history retrieval, and conflict-resolution operations. Every accepted submission creates an immutable whole-visit revision containing the complete scientific payload. A server-generated revision ID identifies that state; its lineage names the base revision supplied by the tablet. The stored submission also retains its idempotency key, author, original payload, and server receipt time.
 
 A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+The store therefore never combines fields from independently submitted revisions. A coordinator may choose one complete revision or deliberately author a complete replacement during resolution, but that judgment is explicit and attributed.
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+## Revision Model
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+- A submission envelope contains a client-generated visit ID, an idempotency key, a complete visit payload, and a base revision ID. The base is absent only when creating a visit.
+- An accepted worker submission produces one immutable revision and one stable acceptance result. The revision retains the submitted payload without normalization that changes scientific meaning.
+- A visit has a resolved head and may have an unresolved conflict set. The resolved head is the last revision accepted or selected without an outstanding conflict. It remains in history while the visit is unresolved but is not presented as a usable current state.
+- The conflict set contains every complete revision that a coordinator must consider, including the formerly resolved head. It has a server-generated conflict token that changes whenever its membership changes.
+- Revision history is append-only. Resolving a conflict changes visit pointers and records an outcome; it does not alter or delete a worker revision.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+## Submission Processing
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types, the plot reference, visit identity, and base revision before acceptance. For a new visit, the first accepted base-less submission becomes its resolved head. A later distinct base-less submission for the same visit is accepted as a competitor rather than treated as an update with inferred lineage.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+For an existing visit, the named base must belong to that visit. When no conflict is open and the base is the resolved head, the accepted revision advances the resolved head. Otherwise, the accepted revision opens or extends a conflict set. Opening a conflict adds both the previously resolved head and the new revision. Extending one adds the new revision without removing any existing competitor, regardless of which historical revision it names as its base.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A retry with the same idempotency key and byte-for-byte identical submission envelope returns the original acceptance result. Reuse of the key with any different envelope value is rejected. Validation and idempotency-key rejection create no revision and do not change conflict state.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Acceptance reports the revision ID and whether the visit is current or unresolved. Acceptance does not imply that an unresolved revision is exportable.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Retrieval
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Visit retrieval returns one of two explicit states:
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+- `current`: the resolved head revision and no unresolved conflict;
+- `unresolved`: the conflict token and metadata for every competing revision, with no usable current payload.
+
+Revision-history retrieval returns immutable revision payloads and lineage, including authorship and receipt metadata. Conflict retrieval must provide complete candidate payloads so review-export can compare revisions without reconstructing states from patches.
+
+## Conflict Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves the complete conflict set visible under one conflict token. The request supplies that token, all contributing worker revision IDs, and either selects one of those revisions or supplies a complete replacement payload. A replacement creates an immutable coordinator-authored resolution revision whose provenance names every contributing revision and the coordinator.
+
+Resolution is atomic. The store accepts it only when the token and contributing revision set exactly match the current conflict. If another submission has changed the set, the resolution is rejected without changing the visit so the coordinator must review the added revision. A successful resolution makes the selected or replacement revision the resolved head, records the outcome and coordinator identity, and closes the conflict.
+
+A later worker submission based on a revision other than the new resolved head opens another conflict; resolving earlier work does not silently suppress an offline edit that arrives afterward.
+
+## Downstream Contracts
+
+Tablet-sync must retain the base revision ID associated with each editable local copy and submit complete visit payloads. It does not need base snapshots or structured field changes for record-store reconciliation.
+
+Review-export must treat an `unresolved` visit as review work and must not treat its prior resolved head as current. Its resolution workflow compares whole revisions and submits the conflict token plus the full contributing set. CSV eligibility and presentation remain owned by review-export.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Preserve complete competing revisions for explicit coordinator resolution | Field-aware three-way merge | Whole revisions preserve scientifically related edits as submitted and make every combination an attributed human judgment. The larger review queue is preferable to maintaining merge rules that can combine related observations without noticing. See [Concurrent Visit Edits](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md). |
+| Resolution concurrency | Require an exact conflict token and contributing set | Resolve a selected subset; serialize submissions behind review | Exact-set resolution prevents a coordinator from unknowingly omitting a revision that arrived during review while allowing offline submissions to continue. |
+
